  "close_rendering": "Here is wisdom. Let the one who has a mind calculate the number of the beast, for it is a number of a man, and its number is six hundred sixty-six.",
  "whats_happening": "The passage about the second beast ends by turning to the reader and setting a task rather than giving an explanation. The number is written out as three ordinary words rather than as a figure, and the verse says it is a number of a man without saying which man. This is the only place in the book that explicitly invites the reader to work something out.",
  "key_words": [
    {
      "surface": "ψηφισάτω",
      "lemma": "ψηφίζω",
      "gloss": "let him calculate",
      "note": "An aorist imperative addressed to a third party. The verb is about reckoning or counting up, and is built on the word for a pebble used in counting.",
      "strongs": "G5585"
    },
    {
      "surface": "ἀνθρώπου",
      "lemma": "ἄνθρωπος",
      "gloss": "of a man",
      "note": "Genitive and without the article, which leaves open whether a particular person or humanity in general is meant.",
      "strongs": "G444"
    },
    {
      "surface": "ἑξακόσιοι",
      "lemma": "ἑξακόσιοι",
      "gloss": "six hundred",
      "note": "The number is spelled out in words here rather than written with letters standing for numerals.",
      "strongs": "G1812"
    }
  ],
  "translation_note": "English versions print 666 as a figure, which the Greek does not. Writing it out as three words is part of what the verse is doing, since the reader is being asked to count rather than to recognise a symbol.",
